<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>World | Deutsche Welle</title><subtitle type="html">World | Deutsche Welle</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-world</id><updated>2026-11-14T15:13:46Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-world" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/english/?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-world" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Ukraine: Zelenskyy says security deal could go to referendum</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d38b80a06ee6f93e9685daa13b45429234b11f4420374ac5644ba74731d49908</id><updated>2026-11-14T15:13:19Z</updated><published>2026-11-14T11:40:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/ukraine-zelenskyy-says-security-deal-could-go-to-referendum/a-79947535--17ea05d7ca0f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/ukraine-zelenskyy-says-security-deal-could-go-to-referendum/a-79947535?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The US has broached the idea of Kyiv giving up the Donbas region in exchange for NATO membership, the Ukrainian president said.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>New facilities in Laos and Cambodia: Is China expanding its military presence in Southeast Asia?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b002d6c0920210f92b60006d4ca2d4aebfd553050a6d32be84b8fc76e3f17311</id><updated>2026-11-14T15:13:21Z</updated><published>2026-11-14T11:24:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/new-facilities-in-laos-and-cambodia-is-china-expanding-its-military-presence-in-southeast-asia/a-79944798--c731be0f2b99.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/new-facilities-in-laos-and-cambodia-is-china-expanding-its-military-presence-in-southeast-asia/a-79944798?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">China has been growing its economic relationships in Southeast Asia. Now new military facilities in Laos and Cambodia are causing smaller countries to worry China's military networks are also expanding.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Microsoft says all services working again</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:795b3e2326f5731b0c7603fe88a848a440ee3cff4b86b1a49072ab924ee7f1fd</id><updated>2026-11-14T15:13:36Z</updated><published>2026-11-14T09:06:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/microsoft-says-all-services-working-again/a-79946887--03062deb25b6.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/microsoft-says-all-services-working-again/a-79946887?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">US tech giant Microsoft says it has fixed problems that led to a global outage of its services. The glitch severely disrupted online communications in businesses and governments for more than 24 hours.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Sudan: Misery worsens amid plummeting aid for displaced people</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b620e32ba18cba0ec15936e6d364e1097821441827045a1d3c5167785d9d73b8</id><updated>2026-11-14T15:13:39Z</updated><published>2026-11-14T08:31:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/sudan-misery-worsens-amid-plummeting-aid-for-displaced-people/a-79938864--624277167dba.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/sudan-misery-worsens-amid-plummeting-aid-for-displaced-people/a-79938864?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Sudan's humanitarian crisis is exacerbated by cuts in international aid. Almost 34 million people are in need as war and drone strikes are making it difficult to deliver aid to the population.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Nigeria: How the pcoming elections are influeced by the rise of AI disinformation</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:40af4db108b6b605271dff8f8ab94167e7908876d3c506bd7a99ab94b58262e5</id><updated>2026-11-14T15:13:43Z</updated><published>2026-11-14T07:54:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/nigeria-how-the-pcoming-elections-are-influeced-by-the-rise-of-ai-disinformation/a-79946897--d58e239555ba.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/nigeria-how-the-pcoming-elections-are-influeced-by-the-rise-of-ai-disinformation/a-79946897?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">As Nigeria inches closer to the 2027 polls, the use of artificial intelligence in the electoral process has prompted questions about integrity and institutional readiness. Can Nigerians trust their democratic processes?</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Espionage accusations: a pressure tactic for Turkish government critics</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d85f12f52091f91d7dfe4141dbbad84412e020d14f9e73bcef21296a8b47ea08</id><updated>2026-11-14T15:13:46Z</updated><published>2026-11-14T07:31:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/espionage-accusations-a-pressure-tactic-for-turkish-government-critics/a-79943768--bbac38306b52.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/espionage-accusations-a-pressure-tactic-for-turkish-government-critics/a-79943768?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">This week's arrest of an employee of a German foundation in Istanbul has put focus back on the Erdogan administration's pressure tactics. The arrest is far from the first of its kind.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Gen Z protests: Do Baby Boomers really have it better than young people?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:460c323409a09a9f4c4db3403d85cf304790a702e830831110c44224ab0f5765</id><updated>2026-11-14T15:12:27Z</updated><published>2026-11-14T06:09:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/gen-z-protests-do-baby-boomers-really-have-it-better-than-young-people/a-79918854--e581c42657a0.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/gen-z-protests-do-baby-boomers-really-have-it-better-than-young-people/a-79918854?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Should young people in Germany be more worried about their future than Baby Boomers were in the 1980s and 1990s? Or should both generations take to the streets and protest together? DW checked the data.</summary><category term="Germany" /></entry><entry><title>SpaceX launches Starship rocket, fails to recover booster</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c073b3b5f8460ee7a7b8c9105a47831f8ba0ea664482508f79f72a62b7fb874a</id><updated>2026-11-14T15:12:27Z</updated><published>2026-11-14T02:24:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/spacex-launches-starship-rocket-fails-to-recover-booster/a-79946091--bd09d60b45a7.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/spacex-launches-starship-rocket-fails-to-recover-booster/a-79946091?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">SpaceX had planned to catch and later reuse both stages of its biggest rocket for the first time. But the booster exploded over the Gulf of Mexico, while the spacecraft continued into orbit.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Tanker collides with cruise ship on Mississippi in New Orleans</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:0a8378890ce2461292cb5fd29d0f74303ecac23c697433352254e7db579eeb1b</id><updated>2026-11-14T15:12:27Z</updated><published>2026-11-13T21:31:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/tanker-collides-with-cruise-ship-on-mississippi-in-new-orleans/a-79945080--29dd618b519d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/tanker-collides-with-cruise-ship-on-mississippi-in-new-orleans/a-79945080?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">A freighter collided with a cruise ship carrying around 130 people in New Orleans, causing the paddlewheel vessel to capsize. More than 30 people were hospitalized, but all passengers survived and were accounted for.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>The dangers of revising Europe's security architecture</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b3fb681feee694d0849cf05d4bf7f4c38557840a982942fd856d7465575f7045</id><updated>2026-11-14T15:12:27Z</updated><published>2026-11-13T17:30:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/the-dangers-of-revising-europe-s-security-architecture/a-79943049--ae25152ee718.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/the-dangers-of-revising-europe-s-security-architecture/a-79943049?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Experts warn against revising Europe's security framework because they say it would mean granting Moscow its longstanding wish of renegotiating postwar security on the continent.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Why does El Salvador's Bukele have troops in Haiti?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a21acfb01cae27bce620a94a43107de38f46421a9f64459d51b3d2c58d13aad0</id><updated>2026-11-14T15:12:27Z</updated><published>2026-11-13T15:37:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/why-does-el-salvador-s-bukele-have-troops-in-haiti/a-79940771--dfce4a53e7c3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/why-does-el-salvador-s-bukele-have-troops-in-haiti/a-79940771?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Haiti's Prime Minister congratulating El Salvador's President for a gang crackdown in Haiti has shone a light on the relationship between the two countries. What's going on, and where does the US stand?</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Iran targeted US radar system in Bahrain</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:0ab6ac15af2eed1afddeee750ed3c28152264ae35a3553ce9de0c6a3ed593f6d</id><updated>2026-11-14T15:12:27Z</updated><published>2026-11-13T15:32:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/iran-targeted-us-radar-system-in-bahrain/a-79942640--6a6890129eef.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/iran-targeted-us-radar-system-in-bahrain/a-79942640?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Iran claimed it struck US radar systems in Bahrain, while the US said it had intercepted the attacks. Clashes continue in the Strait of Hormuz, as the war remains in a state of de-escalation.</summary><category term="News" /></entry></feed>
